        "paragraph": "The total capital expenditure (CapEx) for Hapag-Lloyd in the financial year 2023 was EUR 3,315.4 million. Of this, EUR 3,221.5 million, representing 97.2% of total CapEx, was identified as taxonomy-eligible, down from 98.4% in 2022. The taxonomy-aligned CapEx (A.1) amounted to EUR 214.8 million (6.5%), all of which was for 'Sea and coastal freight water transport, vessels for port operations and auxiliary activities' (CCM 6.10), classified as transitional. This activity was eligible for Climate Change Mitigation and met all six Do No Significant Harm (DNSH) criteria (Climate Change Mitigation, Climate Change Adaptation, Water, Pollution, Circular Economy, and Biodiversity were all 'Y'), with a 16.3% alignment in 2022. No CapEx was categorized as 'enabling'. However, despite this eligibility and contribution, the overall taxonomy-aligned CapEx for 2023 was 0.0%, as Hapag-Lloyd was unable to demonstrate compliance with minimum safeguards for the reporting year. \n\nThe majority of taxonomy-eligible CapEx, EUR 3,006.7 million (90.7%), fell under activities that were taxonomy-eligible but not environmentally sustainable (A.2). This category showed 97.2% eligibility for Climate Change Mitigation and 0.0% for all other substantial contribution criteria. Within A.2, 'Sea and coastal freight water transport, vessels for port operations and auxiliary activities' (CCM 6.10) had two entries: EUR 639.0 million (19.3%), eligible for Climate Change Mitigation and meeting all six DNSH criteria (all 'Y'), with no specified 2022 alignment; and EUR 1,537.0 million (46.4%), eligible for Climate Change Mitigation but not meeting any DNSH criteria (all 'N'), showing a 78.4% alignment in 2022. 'Infrastructure enabling low carbon water transport' (CCM 6.16) amounted to EUR 569.2 million (17.2%), eligible for Climate Change Mitigation but failing all DNSH criteria (all 'N'), with no specified 2022 alignment. \n\nOther taxonomy-eligible but not aligned activities included: 'Retrofitting of sea and coastal freight and passenger water transport' (CCM 6.12) at EUR 43.6 million (1.3%), eligible for Climate Change Mitigation and meeting all DNSH criteria (all 'Y'), with 0.0% alignment in 2022. 'Transport by motorbikes, passenger cars and light commercial vehicles' (CCM 6.5) at EUR 2.2 million (0.1%), eligible for Climate Change Mitigation but failing all DNSH criteria (all 'N'), also with 0.0% alignment in 2022. 'Renovation of existing buildings' (CCM 7.2) accounted for EUR 9.3 million (0.3%), eligible for Climate Change Mitigation but failing all DNSH criteria (all 'N'), with no specified 2022 alignment. Finally, 'Acquisition and ownership of buildings' (CCM 7.7) accounted for EUR 206.4 million (6.2%), eligible for Climate Change Mitigation but failing all DNSH criteria (all 'N'), showing 3.8% alignment in 2022. For all taxonomy-eligible activities in A.2, minimum safeguards were not met ('N'). \n\nEUR 93.8 million, or 2.8% of the total CapEx, was identified as taxonomy-non-eligible.",
